WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:04.550
In the last section, we saw that our new
algorithm, based on the leaderboard and

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:08.850
“cut” method, worked well on a

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:11.280
spectrum that was close to the correct
theoretical spectrum.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.330
But once we had 25% false and missing

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:18.726
masses, it didn't reconstruct the correct
Tyrocidine B1 peptide.

00:00:18.726 --> 00:00:21.290
I want to add some more bad news to that

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:26.360
in this section, which is that these NRPs,
these non-ribosomal peptides,

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:28.760
including Tyrocidine B1,

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:32.330
in practice will contain more
non-standard amino acids

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:35.160
that are outside of the standard
genetic code.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.660
And this is because they're outside of the
Central Dogma.

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:43.170
For example, here is a relative of
Tyrocidine B1 called Tyrocidine B.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:46.610
And it's very similar to Tyrocidine B1
except you'll notice

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:49.780
that in this position, it has an amino
acid called ornithine.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:51.930
This is a non-standard one.

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:55.950
And so to deal with these non-standard
amino acids, because there

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:57.210
are so many of them,

00:00:57.210 --> 00:01:00.080
what bioinformaticians assume,
sometimes,

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:03.780
and what we'll assume here, is that any
integer between 57

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:07.000
and 200 can act as the mass of an amino
acid.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:08.220
 

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:12.700
So we've expanded now our integer mass
alphabet, from

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:17.010
18 masses to 144 amino acids, by making
this assumption.

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:20.880
So then our question is, OK, we expand
our alphabet and

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:23.790
now we have to deal with a larger class of
peptides.

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:26.940
Let's run our LeaderboardCyclopeptideSequencing

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:30.270
algorithm on this expanded
alphabet.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:33.240
To this Spectrum10, with 10% false and
missing masses.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.630
Now remember that before, when we assumed the
standard alphabet,

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:39.480
it reconstructed Tyrocidine B1 correctly.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.250
But here when we expand the alphabet out
to

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:46.402
144 amino acids, the algorithm now
identifies

00:01:46.402 --> 00:01:49.090
the highest scoring peptide as this
peptide.

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:52.340
And it incorporates 98 and 65 into the
peptide.

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:56.920
So it doesn't reconstruct the peptide
correctly,

00:01:56.920 --> 00:02:00.510
and notice that it's brought in two
nonstandard amino acids.

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:04.180
The key point here is that although
Tyrocidine B1

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:08.250
doesn't actually contain any of these
nonstandard amino acids,

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:10.850
we won't know that when we're doing
sequencing experiments.

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:12.840
So we need to take them into account.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:14.990
And in this case, the algorithm would tell
us that

00:02:14.990 --> 00:02:18.080
it has nonstandard amino acids in it, and
that's not good,

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:18.680
because it doesn't.

